{:edb/db-before-t 868 :edb/db-after-t 869 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534181 50 #inst "2026-10-06T08:23:55.886Z" 13194139534181 true] [17592186151901 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $B'$, the image of $B(2,-3)$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$?" 13194139534181 false] [17592186151901 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $B',$ the image of $B(2,-3)$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" 13194139534181 true] [17592186151901 1145 "To find the image of $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTx &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x}$, contains the coordinates of $B'$. Therefore, $B'(3,-5)$." 13194139534181 false] [17592186151901 1145 "To find the image of $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x},$ contains the coordinates of $B'.$ Therefore, $B'(3,-5).$" 13194139534181 true] [17592186151902 1100 "Consider the line $5x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T$?" 13194139534181 false] [17592186151902 1100 "Consider the line $5x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" 13194139534181 true] [17592186151902 1145 "First, let's find two points on the given line. Solving for $y$, we get $y=5x-3$, and substituting $x=0$ and $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x = 0 & → y = 5(0) - 3 =-3, \\\\ x = 1 & → y = 5(1) - 3 = 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(1,2)$.\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(-3,-6)$ and $Q'(-1,3)$.\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}}(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-6) &= (3 - (-6))/(-1 - (-3))(x - (-3)) \\\\\ny + 6 &= \\frac{9}{2}(x + 3) \\\\\n2y + 12 &= 9x + 27 \\\\\n9x - 2y + 15 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534181 false] [17592186151902 1145 "First, let's find two points on the given line. Solving for $y,$ we get $y=5x-3,$ and substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=5(0)-3=-3, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=5(1)-3=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(1,2).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(-3,-6)$ and $Q'(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{3-(-6)}{-1-(-3)}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{9}{2}(x+3) \\\\ 2y+12 & =9x+27 \\\\ 9x-2y+15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534181 true] [17592186151903 1100 "Consider the line $y=3x-2$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T$?" 13194139534181 false] [17592186151903 1100 "Consider the line $y=3x-2$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" 13194139534181 true] [17592186151903 1145 "First, let's find two points on the given line. Substituting $x=0$ and $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x = 0 & → y = 3(0) - 2 =-2, \\\\ x = 1 & → y = 3(1) - 2 = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1)$.\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(0,-2)$ and $Q'(-1,4)$.\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}}(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-2) &= (4 - (-2))/(-1 - 0)(x - 0) \\\\\ny + 2 &= -6x \\\\\n6x + y + 2 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534181 false] [17592186151903 1145 "First, let's find two points on the given line. Substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=3(0)-2=-2, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=3(1)-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(0,-2)$ and $Q'(-1,4).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-0}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =-6x \\\\ 6x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534181 true] [17592186152104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280920 13194139534181 true] [17592186152104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280931 13194139534181 true] [17592186152104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280936 13194139534181 true] [17592186152104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280956 13194139534181 true] [17592186152105 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280925 13194139534181 true] [17592186152105 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280928 13194139534181 true] [17592186152105 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280949 13194139534181 true] [17592186152105 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280952 13194139534181 true] [17592186152106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280942 13194139534181 true] [17592186152106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280945 13194139534181 true] [17592186152106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280963 13194139534181 true] [17592186152106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280970 13194139534181 true] [17592186152106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280977 13194139534181 true] [17592186152692 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534181 true] [17592186152692 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $A^{′}$, the image of $A(-1, 1)$ under the action of $T$?" 13194139534181 false] [17592186152692 1100 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${A}^{′}$, the image of $A(-1,1)$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139534181 true] [17592186152692 1145 "To find the image of $A$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $A:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${A}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${A}^{′}(2,1).$" 13194139534181 true] [17592186152693 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534181 true] [17592186152693 1100 "Consider the line $2x + y - 3 = 0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$?" 13194139534181 false] [17592186152693 1100 "Consider the line $2x+y-3=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" 13194139534181 true] [17592186152693 1145 "First, notice that the line $2x+y-3=0$ passes through the points $A(0,3)$ and $B(1,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(0,6)$ and ${B}^{′}(-2,3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-6 & =\\frac{3-6}{-2-0}(x-0) \\\\ y-6 & =\\frac{3}{2}x \\\\ 2y-12 & =3x \\\\ 3x-2y+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534181 true] [17592186280920 1017 #edb/ref 175921862